<h1>how to get a job with no experience and no degree</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">You build proof of work that recruiters can evaluate in under two minutes. No degree means you skip the credential filter entirely, and no experience means you manufacture the evidence yourself: scored projects, public artifacts, and demonstrable outcomes that make both questions irrelevant.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>The degree reset is real but shallow: <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">46% of employers dropped degree requirements for some roles, yet fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real change</a>. The door is cracked open; you must kick it through with proof.</li>
    <li>An internship cuts underemployment odds by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a>, but you do not need a formal program because self-directed projects with scored feedback serve as internship-equivalent evidence.</li>
    <li>A scored project with a percentile rank against professionals forces a longer look than a credential line item.</li>
    <li>The real barrier is not missing credentials; it is missing artifacts that survive a quick scan.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>why the "no degree, no experience" trap is a category error</h2>
  
  <p>Employers hire outcomes produced by a person in a specific context, not degrees or years, and the degree and experience requirements are lazy proxies adopted because evaluating actual capability is expensive. Your job is to obsolete the proxy, not lament lacking it.</p>
  
  <p>This is where the university filter OS reveals its cruelty: the system trains you to believe credentials accumulate into employability, first the degree, then the internship, then the entry role, but the pipeline is breaking. <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market">Recent graduates now face higher unemployment than the overall workforce</a>, a decades-long pattern reversed, so having the degree is no longer having the key.</p>
  
  <p>The twin disqualifiers, no degree plus no experience, paradoxically free you from playing a losing game. You are not competing with credential-stacked peers for HR-filtered roles; you are targeting the growing minority of hiring managers who have dropped degree requirements and are desperate for verified skill signals. The Burning Glass Institute found that while 46% of employers formally dropped degree requirements, real behavioral change lagged massively, and this gap is your terrain: companies that want skills but do not yet have systems to find them.</p>
  
  <p>Students borrowing for credentials are purchasing a depreciating filter-pass, while you skip the purchase entirely and build the asset that actually appreciates: demonstrated, scored, comparable proof of work.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>the three proof types that replace credentials</h2>
  
  <p>Not all self-made evidence is equal. Recruiters develop antibodies quickly, and "I built a website" is now noise. You need artifacts with three properties: specificity, comparability, and consequence.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Type one: scored work against a professional bar.</strong> This is the zero mechanism, and it is structurally superior to every alternative. You complete a scenario drawn from an actual company workflow, your output is evaluated against what professionals produce, and you receive a percentile rank. A recruiter seeing "scored at 73rd percentile against practicing product managers" understands something concrete, unlike a Coursera certificate that signals you watched videos and passed quizzes. The platform reports over 160 million registered learners, which means the signal-to-noise ratio for completion-based credentials approaches zero.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Type two: public artifacts with engagement metrics.</strong> A GitHub repository with stars and documented issues, a market analysis published on Substack with subscriber growth and comment threads, a redesigned app flow posted to Figma Community with remixes. The metrics are imperfect but harder to fake than a certificate, and they demonstrate distribution sense alongside craft.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Type three: documented outcomes for real entities, even unpaid.</strong> You ran social media for a local business and grew followers 340%, you rebuilt a nonprofit's donation flow and reduced abandonment. You are not "seeking experience" but a practitioner who has not yet been paid. Document the before, the intervention, the after, with numbers wherever possible.</p>
  
  <p>The common thread: each type creates evaluation material that forces a recruiter to judge you on demonstrated capability rather than proxy signals, and the absence of degree and experience becomes invisible because the evidence occupies the entire frame.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>where the actual entry points are hiding</h2>
  
  <p>Most job seekers hunt where competition is thickest: posted roles on LinkedIn and Indeed, filtered by "entry level" that secretly requires two years. The real openings are in channels with higher friction and lower visibility.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Skills-based hiring portals.</strong> Companies using structured assessments often bypass resume screens entirely. TestGorilla's research documents the shift, but the implementation is uneven. Target firms that advertise assessment-first processes, then optimize for scoring high, not for polishing a resume.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Founder-direct outreach.</strong> Early-stage companies lack HR infrastructure, and their founders personally review inbound. Your proof artifact, sent with a single specific observation about their product or market, bypasses every filter. The email subject line is the only gate: "Redesigned your onboarding flow, attached 90-second walkthrough."</p>
  
  <p><strong>Contract-to-hire and project-based bridges.</strong> Platforms like Upwork and Tonic are underrated for this purpose. A $800 project that delivers measurable value becomes a reference, a case study, and a relationship. The pay is insulting relative to full-time roles, but the conversion rate to proper employment is higher than most internship pipelines.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Internal transfers from adjacent roles.</strong> Customer support at a SaaS company is not the dream job, but it is proximity to product, engineering, and sales teams who see your work ethic daily. The path from support to product operations to product management is well-documented because it works, and it requires no degree gate at entry.</p>
  
  <p>The pattern: each channel reduces the importance of credentials by increasing the visibility of actual work, or by placing you inside organizations where relationships substitute for screening.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how to sequence your first 90 days</h2>
  
  <p>Without a degree or experience, you cannot afford scattershot effort. The first 90 days must build cumulative, compounding evidence.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Days 1-14: diagnose and select.</strong> Pick one target role based on accessible evidence, not fantasy. Read fifty job postings. Extract the actual tasks: what does a marketing coordinator do daily? What tools does a junior data analyst use? Map your existing capabilities honestly. Most people have more transferable fragments than they recognize: organizing events is project management, moderating online communities is user research, Excel for personal budgeting is data manipulation.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Days 15-45: build the first scored artifact.</strong> Choose one scenario or project that produces evaluable output. Complete it, submit it for feedback, iterate based on specific critiques. The first version will be embarrassing, the second will be competent, the third will be competitive. The goal is not perfection but a trajectory visible to outsiders.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Days 46-75: publish and distribute.</strong> Place your artifact where the right people encounter it. This means named tags, relevant communities, direct outreach with personalized hooks. Each distribution attempt teaches you something about how your work reads to others. Iterate the packaging, not just the content.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Days 76-90: convert attention into conversation.</strong> Every reply, comment, or inbound message is a lead. Respond with specificity. Offer a fifteen-minute call to walk through your thinking. The offer itself signals confidence that certificates cannot replicate. Close some, ignore most, keep building.</p>
  
  <p>The sequence is deliberately front-loaded with uncomfortable action. Waiting until you feel ready is how you remain permanently unqualified.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what to ignore so you do not waste years</h2>
  
  <p>Certain paths are traps disguised as preparation. Avoid them ruthlessly.</p>
  
  <p><strong>More courses.</strong> The course platform ecosystem, Coursera to Udemy to LinkedIn Learning, teaches you that consumption equals progress, but it does not. The market for "learning" is saturated, and the market for proof is empty. Stop at the minimum viable knowledge and pivot to production.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Certification stacking.</strong> Google Analytics, HubSpot inbound, AWS Cloud Practitioner: each adds a badge, none add evidence of applied judgment. Recruiters know this. The badges signal time spent, not capability exercised.</p>
  
  <p><strong>"Networking" as socializing.</strong> Coffee chats with strangers who have no hiring authority, informational interviews that produce no artifact, following influencers on LinkedIn: these are comfort activities. Network through work worth sharing. The right connections form around evidence, not requests.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Perfectionism about portfolio pieces.</strong> A project you hesitate to show because it is not polished enough is a project that teaches you nothing about audience reaction. Ship rough, gather feedback, improve publicly. The iteration narrative itself becomes part of your story.</p>
  
  <p>The filter OS trained you to believe preparation precedes opportunity. Invert it: opportunity emerges from premature, visible action.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <details>
    <summary>can I really get hired without any formal credentials?</summary>
    <p>Yes, in specific contexts. Companies that have genuinely dropped degree requirements, startups without structured HR processes, and roles where output is immediately evaluable (design, writing, coding, analysis) are all viable. The constraint is your proof quality, not your credential absence. Build evidence that survives scrutiny and the question dissolves.</p>
  </details>
  
  <details>
    <summary>what roles are most accessible without degree or experience?</summary>
    <p>Sales development, customer success, content creation, digital marketing operations, and certain technical support roles have low credential barriers and high output visibility. Avoid roles with licensed requirements (accounting, nursing, law) or where large-company HR mandates degree screens (management consulting, investment banking).</p>
  </details>
  
  <details>
    <summary>how do I handle the "education" section on my resume?</summary>
    <p>Omit it entirely if blank, or replace with a "self-directed learning" section listing specific projects and their outcomes. Better yet, lead with a "selected work" section before any traditional resume categories. The goal is to make the recruiter forget to look for education because the evidence commands attention first.</p>
  </details>
  
  <details>
    <summary>should I explain my situation in cover letters?</summary>
    <p>No. Never apologize for or justify missing credentials. Lead with what you have built and the specific value you bring to this company's stated problem. If they ask in interview, answer briefly and redirect to your evidence. Defensiveness is the only real disqualifier.</p>
  </details>
  
  <details>
    <summary>how long until this strategy produces results?</summary>
    <p>Three to six months of consistent artifact-building and distribution typically generates initial conversations. The timeline compresses with focus and elongates with perfectionism or credential-seeking detours. The first role may be below your target; treat it as paid proof-building for the next move.</p>
  </details>
</section>

